            <ArticleTitle>Societal solutions to childhood origins of coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.tem.2012.10.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1043-2760(12)00180-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Childhood obesity and associated risks result in premature cardiovascular damage and disease with a consequent, large burden to society. There are causes for childhood obesity that are rooted in the socioeconomic milieu. Interventions that are population-based, and aimed towards prevention as opposed to treatment, are likely to be most effective in curtailing childhood obesity. Reforms to federal and state managed social welfare programs provide a compelling opportunity to affect the course and consequences of childhood obesity.</AbstractText>
